# Aegis class-subsumption rules — kept SEPARATE from `aegis-rules.ttl` on purpose.
#
# `aegis-rules.ttl` holds two transitive closures (`depends_on`, `runs_on`) whose
# blast radius is the whole graph. This file holds only vocabulary-convergence
# rules, so it can be loaded on its own without also materialising those.
#
# Load as its own shape set (the reasoner reads every loaded set):
#   POST /shapes {"action":"load","name":"class-subsumption","turtle":"<this file>"}
#
# The ruleset is a snapshot taken at server STARTUP (src/server.rs) — loading
# through /shapes does not take effect until quipu restarts. Not a bug, but it
# is the difference between "loaded" and "live", and this workstream has been
# bitten by that gap five times.

@prefix rule:  <http://quipu.local/rule#> .
@prefix ex:    <http://aegis.gastown.local/rules/> .

# ── Commit ⊑ GitCommit ────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# aegis-h69po merged `aegis:Commit` into `aegis:GitCommit` with
# `owl:equivalentClass`. OWL materialization is ONE-SHOT at ontology load and
# nothing re-runs it, so that merge was a snapshot: correct once, then stale.
# Once aegis-xvejp fixed commit-ingest.py to emit the canonical `GitCommit`,
# the divergence did not close — it REVERSED, and both sides began to grow.
#
# Expressed as a rule instead, the reactive reasoner re-derives it on every
# write, so it holds continuously rather than at one instant (aegis-jgxas).
#
# ⚠ ONE DIRECTION, DELIBERATELY. The symmetric encoding — adding
# `type(?x, Commit) :- type(?x, GitCommit)` — is the obvious reading of
# "equivalent class" and it is a TRAP. The two rules become each other's
# support: `World::load` reads `current_facts()`, which does not distinguish
# base facts from derived ones, and truth maintenance is re-derive-and-diff
# with no support sets (full TMS is deferred — see the reactive module docs).
# A retracted base type is then never un-derived. Measured as a STABLE,
# non-converging fixpoint, not a transient one; pinned by
# `probe_mutual_class_equivalence_under_retraction` in evaluate_tests.rs.
#
# Subsumption converges and stays retractable. Equivalence does not.
#
# What this means for the numbers, so nobody reads the result as a half-fix:
# every `Commit` also becomes a `GitCommit`, so `GitCommit` is the COMPLETE
# population and Commit-only goes to 0. `GitCommit`-only stays non-zero and
# GROWS — those are entities carrying only the canonical vocabulary, which is
# the desired end state, not divergence. Query `GitCommit`.
ex:git_commit_subsumes_commit a rule:Rule ;
    rule:id "git_commit_subsumes_commit" ;
    rule:head "<http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type>(?x, <http://aegis.gastown.local/ontology/GitCommit>)" ;
    rule:body "<http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type>(?x, <http://aegis.gastown.local/ontology/Commit>)" .
